    "created_at": "2018-02-16T20:04:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-02-16T20:04:27Z",
    "body": "The confusing thing here is this part of the spec:\r\n\r\n> If the attribute value matches none of the given keywords, but the attribute has an invalid value default, then the attribute represents that state. **Otherwise, if the attribute value matches none of the keywords but there is a missing value default state defined, then that is the state represented by the attribute.** Otherwise, there is no default, and invalid values mean that there is no state represented.\r\n\r\nHere you can see that the missing value default acts as a sort of \"backup\" invalid value default.\r\n\r\nThe work to do here is to canvas all places in the spec that specify a missing value default, and make sure that they *also* specify an invalid value default. Then, additionally, remove the fallback sentence, since it is not longer necessary.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
